# {{ .name }}

An Intropy transactional integration. Each run sweeps the `{{ .fromPort }}`
source port and queues every file on the internal topic
`internal-{{ .appId }}`; a send pipeline (deserialize, validate, transform,
serialize, send) takes each queued file and writes the result to the
`{{ .toPort }}` destination port.
{{- if .empty }}

The pipeline steps were scaffolded empty: each throws
`NotImplementedException` until it is implemented.
{{- end }}

## How a run flows

```
scheduled run: sweep {{ .fromPort }} ──▶ per file:
   │
   ▼
publish to internal-{{ .appId }} ──▶ source file deleted
   │
   ▼  (per delivery from internal-{{ .appId }})
{{- if .empty }}
Deserializer ──▶ Extractor ──▶ Validator ──▶ Transformer ──▶ Serializer ──▶ Sender
{{- else }}
Deserializer ──▶ idempotency check ──▶ Extractor ──▶ Validator
   ──▶ Transformer ──▶ Serializer ──▶ Sender
{{- end }}
   │
   ▼
{{ .toPort }} gets {{ if .empty }}the result{{ else }}{orderId}.json{{ end }}
```

The framework owns the receive side (sweep, publish, delete); the component
owns the send pipeline. A file that can't be read or published stays for the
next run.
{{- if not .empty }}

Off the happy path in the send pipeline:

- **Invalid data:** the `Validator` routes a business incident and the message
  is consumed without writing.
- **Duplicate:** the idempotency check cancels it; nothing is written.
- **Destination write failure:** the `Sender` routes a business incident and
  the message is redelivered once the destination is back.
{{- end }}

## Build and test

```bash
task build
task test
```

The job does not run standalone. Run it with the rest of the system from the
system host (`task run` there).

## Container image

```bash
docker build -t {{ .appId }} .
```

See `AGENTS.md` for the component's names, ports and key files, and
<https://docs.intropy.io> for how transactional integrations work.
